Your Nevada landlord has **30 days**, not 90. That's different from your landlord's note, so I'm not going to repeat the 90-day figure. Here's what PropInspect's Nevada guide says:

- **Deadline:** Within 30 days after the lease ends and you move out, the landlord must either return your deposit or give you a written, itemized list of what they're deducting and why.
- **Penalty for missing it:** If the landlord doesn't return the deposit on time, a court can order them to pay back the whole deposit, plus damages of up to that same amount again. That means up to 2x the deposit in total.
- **Deposit cap:** All security together can't be more than 3 months' rent. That includes any prepaid last month's rent.
- **Move-in record:** Every written lease must provide for a signed record of the unit's inventory and condition. Photos aren't required.

The 30-day clock starts when you've actually moved out and given up possession. If your landlord sticks to the 90-day claim, you can point them to the statute below.

